  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday there is an upper-level trough over Montana with multiple vort \nmaxes. Through the day today it will continue to snow across North-\ncentral and Central Montana enhanced by tropical moisture from \nTropical Storm Rachel. Today across Southwestern Montana in the \nmountains there will be snow and in the lower-elevations there will \nbe a rain/snow mix. There remains uncertainty in how far south the \ncolder air behind the cold front will make it. This will affect \nwhich lower-elevation areas see any snow during the day today. \nSee the Forecast Confidence and Scenarios section for details. \nToday most areas of North-central, and Southwestern Montana will \nstruggle to get above freezing. Sunday night snow-levels will \nlower across Southwestern Montana. This will bring primarily snow \nto all of the lower-elevations of Southwestern Montana Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. Across North-central and Central \nMontana snow will continue Sunday night through Monday morning. On\nMonday the upper- level trough sinks further south. This will \nallow temperatures to warm up a little on Monday. \n\nOn Tuesday and Wednesday upper-level flow over Montana is weak. This \nwill allow temperatures to warm up a few degrees each day. However \nthey will remain below seasonal averages. On Thursday an upper-level \ntrough briefly moves over Montana. This will slightly cool \ntemperatures and bring precipitation chances to the area. On Friday \nand Saturday an upper-level ridge moves over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This warm temperatures and dry weather to \nthe area on Friday. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFor snow in the Southwestern Montana valleys, it will depend on how \nfast cold air makes it down there this morning. In addition with \nwarm road temperatures most of the snow that falls in the \nSouthwestern Montana valleys will melt through the day today.\nAdditionally, snow accumulations at all lower-elevations of \nNorth- central, Central, and Southwestern will vary drastically \nfrom grassy/elevated surfaces to roadways due to warm road \ntemperatures. The greatest impacts for any snowfall will be early \nthis morning and Sunday night into early Monday morning. All \nsnowfall will be wet and heavy. This means that it only will take \na few inches of snow to cause power outages and/or break tree \nbranches. The tree branches that are most susceptible to breaking \nare those that still have leaves. Currently the current Winter \nWeather Products are capturing the current situation well. The one\nexception is the Gallatin Valley. Most of the Hi-res guidance is \npredicting over 6 inches of snowfall with several inches of snow \ndepth change in the Gallatin Valley Sunday evening into Monday \nmorning. As a result the Gallatin Valley was upgraded to a Winter \nStorm Warning from 6 PM Sunday to 9 AM Monday. \n\nToday across the North-central Montana plains along and east of I-15 \nthere is a 30 - 70% chance for high temperatures of 36 degrees or \ncolder. Tuesday morning across the North-central Montana plains \nalong and east of I-15 and across the valleys of Southwestern \nMontana there is a 50 - 80% chance for temperatures of 20 degrees or \ncolder. -IG",
